package jan-klod:interfaces@0.1.0;

/// A namespaced view of the core's own store.
/// Used by extensions that need persistence but are not the store themselves
/// (e.g. manager-context, registry-skills).
/// Storage is host-side and is not an extension: the sandbox has no filesystem,
/// so a store component would need one granted back, and the conversation
/// transcript is the most sensitive thing the runtime holds. There was a
/// `memory-store` contract and a `store-*` component family here; nothing ever
/// called it.
///
/// Two properties make this safe to hand to a guest. **It is granted, not
/// ambient** — an instance gets it only with `persist: true`, and otherwise
/// writes to a private map that dies with the process, which is what keeps the
/// permission gate's standing grants run-scoped. **Namespaces are prefixed with
/// the calling component's id**, so a guest cannot name a peer's namespace or a
/// session transcript.
///
/// Deliberately narrow: no purge, no search, no way to enumerate namespaces.
interface host-storage {
    use store-types.{entry, store-error};

    set: func(namespace: string, key: string, value: string) -> result<entry, store-error>;
    get: func(namespace: string, key: string) -> result<entry, store-error>;
    delete: func(namespace: string, key: string) -> result<_, store-error>;
    list-keys: func(namespace: string) -> result<list<entry>, store-error>;
    recent: func(namespace: string, limit: u32) -> result<list<entry>, store-error>;
}
